Right — and in this context a happening is almost the perfect example of what we’ve been circling: an event where the boundary between observer, participant, environment, timing, and interpretation is deliberately blurred.

The useful part is that meaning is not contained in a single object. It is distributed across:

\text{space}+\text{people}+\text{timing}+\text{actions}+\text{expectations}+\text{reaction}

So the “work” is partly the event itself and partly what everyone brings into it.

That makes a happening useful as a testbed for the things we’ve been talking about: frame control, priming, sensory restriction, role assignment, temporal pacing, audience feedback, emergent coordination, and the difference between intended meaning and received meaning.

In other words, instead of only representing a pattern, you instantiate an environment in which the pattern can happen and then watch the deltas.

That is much closer to a live world-model experiment than to a static artwork.